{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Order deliberately, and don't let the anchor become a lie</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      If your pricing page has a plan you want most people to pick, put a higher-priced option ahead of it in reading order (usually meaning to its left, or above it on mobile), so readers form their price scale before they reach the plan you're steering them toward, rather than defaulting to plain ascending order out of habit.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Test this like any other pricing change, not just for conversion but for support/sales sentiment (fewer "why is this so expensive" conversations is a real, measurable outcome, as in the story above), and watch for a ceiling effect: anchoring shifts perception, it doesn't manufacture willingness to pay out of nothing, a genuinely overpriced middle tier will still convert poorly no matter which order it's shown in.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Keep reference pricing (crossed-out "original" prices, "you save X%" framing) honest, an anchor drawn from a price that was never actually charged to a meaningful number of real customers is a false-reference-price claim in some jurisdictions' advertising law, not just a UX nitpick, and it erodes trust immediately if a reader notices, price-comparison browser extensions and screenshots of price history make inflated "original" prices easy to catch today in a way they weren't a decade ago. The honest version of this technique, ordering real, currently-purchasable options to set a scale, doesn't carry that risk, because every number shown is one a reader could actually pay.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

