{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">The first price a reader sees sets the scale they judge every other price against — the same middle-tier price can read as expensive or reasonable purely based on what came before it.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Order pricing tiers deliberately: put a higher anchor ahead of the plan you want most people to choose, rather than defaulting to plain cheapest-first order.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">Keep any anchor honest — ordering real, purchasable options is legitimate framing, but a crossed-out "original price" that was never actually charged is a false reference point, not just a UX technique, and it's increasingly easy for readers to catch.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
